WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
Film Courage: Can you take us to the first day as the
showrunner for HOME IMPROVEMENT? What was

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.010
that like?
How are you interacting with the writers?

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:12.900
Bruce Ferber, Author/Showrunner: Well I knew all the writers. I had either
worked with them in previous years or had

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:18.449
hired them myself and chances are if I
hired them myself I had worked with them

00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:25.320
maybe on other shows so that was all
easygoing but you know home improvement

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:31.260
was like a juggernaut it was a huge show
and it was a huge responsibility to be

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:36.660
the showrunner and it was my first job
as a showrunner once I found out that I

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:43.590
had the job I had spent a ton of time
prepping for that firsthand job about

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:47.730
how I would handle it what I would do
what my approach to show running would

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:53.309
be and I was lucky because I had had a
good mentor the showrunner before me was

00:00:53.309 --> 00:01:00.030
Elliot Shoenman (Maude, The Cosby Show, Annie McGuire, etc.) and he had a very
precise way of doing it and so I took a

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:08.220
lot from him and my goal in running a
show was to get people excited about

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:16.320
their own ideas everybody else's ideas
and really make it a fun room and when

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.740
you make it a fun room and you value the
other writers ideas great stuff can

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:27.780
happen and so because I had been in the
worst of writers rooms and the worst of

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:32.640
writers rooms go like this where they
have eight writers and one showrunner

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.020
the showrunner wants to rewrite
everything so that all the showrunner

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.970
wants is people around to spit out stuff
and and kind of tell them how great he

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:48.869
is and this happens way too often and at
the end of the day no matter how much

00:01:48.869 --> 00:01:52.259
work they do in that room the showrunner
is going to take it home by himself and

00:01:52.259 --> 00:01:59.130
rewrite every word and so you have a
demoralized staff I had no interest in

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:03.930
having the moralized staff I wanted
other people's contributions because in

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.390
comedy
that's what makes it better you know

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:13.380
just one person alone can really make it
as good as a group there are exceptions

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:18.500
this show fleabag is apparently written
by one person and she's very talented

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:24.330
but who knows maybe fleabag would be
even better if she had a staff you don't

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:29.400
know so how long we the showrunner for
office three years okay

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:34.020
and what was the latest you and your
group worked your team of writers you

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:38.790
know there would be occasional really
late nights and for me for us a really

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:45.870
late night might be midnight there are
rooms that go later than that and but we

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:50.820
always my kind of the thing that I
strove for is a showrunner

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:56.190
was no weekends no weekends in the room
because I had a family I wanted

00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:01.500
to spend time with them and when I was
doing shows even working for other

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.780
people I didn't really mind that much
working the late nights during the week

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:13.100
if I could get those two days off with
my family so that was that was important

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:18.120
would you also look at feedback from the
audience and how would you integrate oh

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:23.370
it was fantastic they had come up before
I even gone the show they had this whole

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:28.590
system now nobody else did this at the
time for the Wednesday run-through we

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:33.450
would read the script on Monday run
through the show on Tuesday afternoon

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:37.770
and then run through it again for the
network on Wednesday afternoon and when

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:42.270
we did our run-through we would bring in
20 people from the universal tour in

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:50.490
folding chairs to watch the run-through
so those those people told us what was

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:55.260
